insert_after <<PLAN
# Objective and Context

"Write a plan to set up scaffolding for the prototype. The goal is to get to the point where you have an input box, the user can type in a message, and the agent can stream a response."

Per `notes/prototype.md`: an agentic harness where the user types, the agent responds and (eventually) uses tools. Scaffolding is ripped from two existing repos:

- `~/src/magenta.nvim/node/core/src/providers/` - the provider/inference abstraction. We borrow the *type vocabulary*, not the code. Nothing from `thread.ts`, `thread-api.ts`, `thread-supervisor.ts`, or `agent.ts` (which imports both) may be depended on.
- `~/src/gatherus/main/` - client/server split, vite, vitest, vamp.

Key entities we are mirroring (exact shapes at `magenta.nvim/node/core/src/providers/provider-types.ts`):

- `StreamingBlock` - `{type:"text",text} | {type:"thinking",thinking} | {type:"tool_use",id,name,inputJson}`. The unit of streaming progress.
- `RequestUpdate` - `{type:"streaming-block",streamingBlock} | {type:"block-finished"} | {type:"retry-scheduled",retry} | {type:"attempt-started"}`. We keep only the first two for now.
- `InferenceRequest` - `{promise: Promise<RequestResult>, abort(): void}`.
- `RequestResult` - `{type:"tool_use",requested} | {type:"stopped",stopReason} | {type:"aborted"} | {type:"error",error}`.
- `NativeInferenceManager` - owns the native `Anthropic.MessageParam[]` array; `appendUserMessage`, `appendToolResults`, `sendRequest(onUpdate)`.
- `AgentInput` - text/image/document content the user submits. We start with text only.

Magenta's `AnthropicInferenceManager` is 1240 lines (retry, OAuth/keychain auth, cache control, token counting, conversion caching). We do not port it; we write ~150 lines against `client.messages.stream()` with the same interface shape, so the real thing can be swapped in later if the prototype outgrows it.

Relevant files (all new):

- `package.json`, `vite.config.ts`, `biome.json`, `tsconfig.json` - root workspace, modeled on gatherus.
- `packages/iso/protocol.ts` - the client<->server websocket message union, shared by both sides.
- `packages/backend/app.ts` - fastify entry, listens on 3000.
- `packages/backend/create-app.ts` - app factory (no listen), so tests can drive it.
- `packages/backend/socket.ts` - `GET /api/socket`, one socket per browser tab, holds the session.
- `packages/backend/session.ts` - per-socket conversation: owns the inference manager, runs the loop, emits frames.
- `packages/backend/inference.ts` - the minimal Anthropic streaming manager.
- `packages/backend/agent-loop.ts` - the turn loop.
- `packages/frontend/index.html`, `main.ts`, `vamp.ts` (copied verbatim from gatherus), `transcript.ts`, `composer.ts`, `socket-client.ts`.

# Design

Data flows in one loop:

composer input -> `dispatch({type:"send",text})` -> socket-client sends `{type:"send",text}` -> backend `Session.send()` -> `agentLoop` -> `manager.sendRequest(onUpdate)` -> Anthropic SDK stream events -> `onUpdate(RequestUpdate)` -> server frames over the socket -> client reducer mutates the last assistant message in place -> `mainView.sync(state)`.

The transcript is the single source of truth on the client and a mirror of the server's message array. The server never sends the whole transcript; it sends deltas. Both sides agree on the invariant that a `block-start` opens a block on the tail assistant message and `block-delta` appends to the open block. This mirrors magenta's `StreamingBlock` accumulation exactly, which is why the client can hold a partially-built block without any special "pending" state.

Why websocket rather than SSE, given the request is one-directional streaming: the prototypes in `notes/prototype.md` (highlight-to-ask, agent-suggested review points, domain map) all need the client to push out-of-band messages into a live session, and gatherus already has the `@fastify/websocket` pattern to copy. Paying for it now is cheaper than migrating later.

State is in-memory and per-socket. Closing the tab discards the conversation, as specified. `Session` is a plain class held in the socket's closure - there is no session registry, no reconnect.

Tools: `ProviderToolSpec[]` is threaded through `InferenceOptions` and `RequestResult` can be `tool_use`, but the executor is a stub that returns an error result for any tool. This keeps the loop's shape honest without porting any tools.

## Interfaces

`packages/iso/protocol.ts`:

```ts
export type ClientMessage =
  | { type: "send"; text: string }
  | { type: "abort" };

export type ServerFrame =
  | { type: "message-start"; role: "assistant" }
  | { type: "block-start"; block: StreamingBlock }
  | { type: "block-delta"; delta: BlockDelta }
  | { type: "block-finished" }
  | { type: "turn-finished"; stopReason: StopReason }
  | { type: "error"; message: string };

export type StreamingBlock =
  | { type: "text"; text: string }
  | { type: "thinking"; thinking: string }
  | { type: "tool_use"; id: string; name: string; inputJson: string };

export type BlockDelta =
  | { type: "text"; text: string }
  | { type: "thinking"; thinking: string }
  | { type: "input_json"; partialJson: string };

export type StopReason = "end_turn" | "max_tokens" | "stop_sequence" | "aborted";
```

`packages/backend/inference.ts`:

```ts
export type AgentInput = { type: "text"; text: string };

export type RequestUpdate =
  | { type: "streaming-block"; streamingBlock: StreamingBlock }
  | { type: "block-delta"; delta: BlockDelta }
  | { type: "block-finished" };

export type RequestedTool = { id: string; name: string; input: unknown };

export type RequestResult =
  | { type: "tool_use"; requested: RequestedTool[] }
  | { type: "stopped"; stopReason: StopReason }
  | { type: "aborted" }
  | { type: "error"; error: Error };

export type InferenceRequest = { promise: Promise<RequestResult>; abort(): void };

export interface InferenceOptions {
  model: string;
  systemPrompt: string;
  tools: ProviderToolSpec[];
}

export class InferenceManager {
  constructor(client: Anthropic, options: InferenceOptions);
  readonly messages: ReadonlyArray<Anthropic.MessageParam>;
  appendUserMessage(content: AgentInput[]): void;
  appendToolResults(results: ReadonlyMap<string, ToolResult>): void;
  sendRequest(onUpdate: (u: RequestUpdate) => void): InferenceRequest;
}
```

Note the divergence from magenta: magenta folds deltas into the `StreamingBlock` it hands to `onUpdate` (it re-emits the whole accumulated block). We emit `block-delta` instead so the wire carries O(response) bytes rather than O(response^2).

`packages/backend/agent-loop.ts`:

```ts
export type ToolExecutor = (requests: ReadonlyArray<RequestedTool>) =>
  Promise<ReadonlyMap<string, ToolResult>>;

export function runAgentLoop(deps: {
  manager: InferenceManager;
  onUpdate: (u: RequestUpdate) => void;
  executeTools: ToolExecutor;
}, input: AgentInput[]): { promise: Promise<RequestResult>; abort(): void };
```

Client state:

```ts
type Message = { role: "user" | "assistant"; blocks: StreamingBlock[] };
type State = { messages: Message[]; status: "idle" | "streaming"; error?: string };
```

## Invariants

- `block-delta` and `block-finished` are only ever sent while a block is open on the tail assistant message; the client may assert this rather than defend against it.
- The client appends the user's message optimistically on send, and the server never echoes it back - the two arrays stay aligned because a send is rejected while `status === "streaming"`.
- `sendRequest` never resolves with a rejected promise; all failures come back as `{type:"error"}`. The socket handler therefore has one error path.
- Aborting a request aborts that request only, and leaves `manager.messages` with a complete assistant message (magenta uses `ABORT_MARKER_TEXT` for this; we append a synthetic text block) so the next turn's message array is still valid for the API.
- The API key is read from `ANTHROPIC_API_KEY` on the server and never crosses the wire.

# Stages

## workspace scaffolding

- Goal: `npm run dev` serves an empty page at 5173 proxying `/api/` to a fastify server on 3000; `npm test` and `npm run typecheck` both run and pass.
- Work: root `package.json` with `packages/*` workspaces, `vite.config.ts` copied from gatherus (drop the MPA/`spaFallback` bits - single page), `biome.json`, per-package tsconfigs, `packages/iso` with `protocol.ts`, `packages/backend/{app,create-app}.ts` with a `/api/health` route, `packages/frontend/{index.html,main.ts}` plus `vamp.ts` copied verbatim.
- Tests:
  - A backend test builds the app via `createApp()` and asserts `/api/health` responds - this proves the factory/entry split works without `listen`.

## streaming inference

- Goal: a node script can construct an `InferenceManager`, send "count to five", and observe text deltas arriving in order, terminating in `{type:"stopped"}`.
- Work: `packages/backend/inference.ts` and `agent-loop.ts`. `@anthropic-ai/sdk` at the version magenta uses (`^0.115.0`).
- Tests:
  - The hard part is the SDK's event shape, not our bookkeeping. Follow magenta's approach in `providers/mock-anthropic-client.ts`: a fake client whose `messages.stream()` replays a scripted `content_block_start`/`delta`/`stop` sequence, so the tests assert our translation into `RequestUpdate` rather than restating it.
  - Two text blocks in one response produce two `streaming-block` opens and two `block-finished`.
  - A `tool_use` stop resolves to `{type:"tool_use"}` with parsed input, and the loop calls `executeTools` then issues a second request - the tool round-trip is where the message array can get corrupted, so assert on the resulting `manager.messages`.
  - Abort mid-stream resolves `{type:"aborted"}` and leaves a well-formed assistant message.
  - One test hitting the real API, skipped unless `ANTHROPIC_API_KEY` is set, to catch drift between the mock and the SDK.

## socket transport

- Goal: a websocket client can connect to `/api/socket`, send `{type:"send"}`, and receive the frame sequence.
- Work: `packages/backend/socket.ts` (heartbeat + buffered-message pattern from `gatherus/packages/backend/sheets/socket.ts`, minus all auth), `session.ts` mapping `RequestUpdate` -> `ServerFrame`.
- Tests:
  - Drive a real socket against a listening test app with the mock client injected, and assert the full ordered frame sequence for one turn. The value here is the wiring, so test it end to end rather than unit-testing the mapper.
  - A `send` arriving while a turn is in flight is rejected with an `error` frame and does not corrupt the message array.

